<Base title="About" description="What tharavad is for.">
  <h1>About</h1>

  <p>
    We love computers. We hate the part of the internet that only performs
    caring about them.
  </p>

  <p>
    Tharavad is a public server for people who want a real box: a place to
    install things, break things, deploy something dumb at 2am, and learn
    without waiting for permission from a platform. It is not a course. It is
    not a vibe. It is hardware and software that still answers when you SSH in.
  </p>

  <h3>TinkerHub brought the iron</h3>

  <p>
    Credit where it is due:
    <a href="https://tinkerhub.org">TinkerHub</a> bought a server so we could
    mess with it without a five-year roadmap, then open the cycles to the
    community instead of hoarding a lonely lab toy. They supplied the cruft-free
    hardware. We frob the bits, mung the config until it boots, twiddle DNS and
    guests, and try not to lose the root partition on a Friday night.
  </p>

  <p>
    The deal is simple and slightly unserious on purpose. Real metal and a
    public front door go in. Out comes a place where more than one nerd can have
    a shell. If that sounds like “we bought a box so people could have a
    playground,” yes. That is the gag, except the playground has SSH keys and
    git history instead of slides.
  </p>

  <p>
    So when you land a VM here, you are not on a private vanity rack. You are on
    infrastructure TinkerHub was willing to fund so the learning stays
    hands-on: less slide deck, more <code>ssh</code>, more “why is this unit
    failed,” more shipping something small that actually runs.
  </p>

  <h3>Where we are</h3>

  <p>
    The default internet today is rented. Your “server” is someone else’s
    control panel. Your “community” is a feed optimized to keep you scrolling.
    Tutorials are infinite, and so is noise. A lot of people who could have
    become dangerous with a shell never get past a signup wall, a credit card,
    or the feeling that the real rooms are closed.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is a waste. The tools are better than ever. What is scarce is a place
    that treats you like a peer instead of a user metric. Forums used to do
    that. University labs used to do that. This is one more corner.
  </p>

  <h3>What we can do here</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Get a <strong>real NixOS guest</strong> under your name, not a line in
      someone else’s passwd file.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>SSH from anywhere</strong> with a normal key:
      <code>you@you.tharavad.xyz</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Publish a small service</strong> if you want:
      <code>http://you.tharavad.xyz</code> on a port you declare in git.
    </li>
    <li>
      Leave a trail that matters: membership is a PR. The history is readable.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Longer rants live under <a href="/blog/">Notes</a> so this page stays a
    door, not a dump. Start with
    <a href="/blog/people-we-stand-on/">People we stand on</a> and
    <a href="/blog/on-http/">On HTTP</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Membership is boring on purpose. You open a pull request with your SSH key.
    Someone reviews it. After merge, you get a guest under your name. Your mess
    stays in your guest. Shape of the machine:
    <a href="https://github.com/tinkerhub0/mothership">mothership</a>. Shell:
    <a href="/join">Join</a>.
  </p>

